{
 "text": "Fifth, the hash function itself is fixed. A C-I-D doesn't say \"this was made with Shaw five-twelve.\" It just is Shaw five-twelve.\n\nSome systems instead put a small label at the front of every name saying which algorithm was used, so they can switch to a new one later. That flexibility is real, but it isn't free. The same content ends up with different names under different algorithms, and every implementation has to handle every option.\n\nTwo fifty-six tee takes the other side of that bet: one algorithm, one name per content, and nothing to negotiate. If Shaw five-twelve ever did start to crack, the remedy would be a new scheme, and the history we looked at suggests there would be years of warning to make that move.",
 "text_sha256": "c9e582069cb2155bd319dd461435c1e225ab6556321f86a50b15b9752ab3c3e8",
 "voice_id": "qCYgSspmts8E3GyY0tyz",
